WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:12.138
A robot can only move right or down across a floor map with obstacles. How many routes reach the far corner? Grid dynamic programming fills the answer cell by cell.

00:00:13.338 --> 00:00:14.266
Pause and think.

00:00:14.666 --> 00:00:18.544
Which cells does each grid cell depend on for right-or-down paths?

00:00:18.944 --> 00:00:26.259
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:27.458 --> 00:00:33.983
Grid dynamic programming fills a table cell by cell, where each cell only looks at neighbors already filled in.

00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:39.420
Picture a city with streets in a grid, and you may only walk right or down.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:46.383
How many ways reach a corner? Exactly the ways to reach the corner above plus the ways to reach the corner to the left.

00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:50.641
You never recount a route: you write the number on each corner and reuse it.

00:00:51.091 --> 00:00:53.297
That written number is the D P table.

00:00:53.375 --> 00:01:07.678
New word: D P. Dynamic Programming: solve a problem by storing answers to smaller sub-problems and reusing them; also DisplayPort. Picture it like this: Writing intermediate sums in a notebook so you never redo them.

00:01:10.225 --> 00:01:13.487
Here is the unique paths table for a three by four grid.

00:01:13.937 --> 00:01:17.037
The top row and left column have only one way each.

00:01:17.487 --> 00:01:19.867
Every other cell is up plus left.

00:01:20.317 --> 00:01:23.417
The bottom right corner holds ten, the number of routes.

00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:27.820
Maximal square uses the same idea with a minimum.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:36.095
A cell can be the bottom right corner of a square of side k only if its up, left and diagonal neighbors all allow side k minus one.

00:01:36.545 --> 00:01:39.831
So dp equals one plus the minimum of those three.

00:01:40.281 --> 00:01:44.495
The biggest value in the table is the side of the biggest square of ones.

00:01:45.845 --> 00:01:47.912
A memory trick interviewers like.

00:01:48.362 --> 00:01:52.785
Each row only needs the row above, so keep one row and a saved diagonal.

00:01:53.235 --> 00:01:56.684
Memory drops from rows times columns to just columns.

00:01:57.134 --> 00:02:01.383
Always say the recurrence, the base cases and the fill order before coding.

00:02:02.733 --> 00:02:09.525
Define the cell, write the recurrence from finished neighbors, fill row by row, and shrink to one row if asked.

00:02:09.507 --> 00:02:10.761
Back to the question.

00:02:11.161 --> 00:02:15.039
Which cells does each grid cell depend on for right-or-down paths?

00:02:15.439 --> 00:02:17.563
The cell above and the cell to the left.

00:02:18.763 --> 00:02:19.634
Quick recap.

00:02:20.034 --> 00:02:22.484
Each cell uses already-filled neighbours.

00:02:22.884 --> 00:02:23.708
Fill in order.

00:02:24.108 --> 00:02:26.151
Often one row of memory is enough.
